// The `gen` lowering — the third of the three, and the first that has to **create a type**.
//
// `./desugar.wac` is the `try` pass: it rewrites statements into other statements. The `union`
// lowering rewrites types into other types. This one turns a function into a struct and a `step`
// function, and the struct did not exist in the source.
//
// **Rewritten twice on 2026-09-05.** The first version ended at a signature — *"There is no `Decl` in
// this tree, so this signature is the end of the file."* — and `./decl.wac` was written for that. The
// second said the pass was still half-blocked, because a `match` over a union cannot bind. That was
// wrong, the whole pass is writable, and how the claim survived three commits is §2.

import { Vec } from "core";
import { Expr, Tok, Span, Ty } from "./ast.wac";
import { Stmt } from "./stmt.wac";
import { Decl, DeclKind, Func, StructDecl, Field, Param } from "./decl.wac";

/** One local that outlives a suspension. `asyncplan.wac` already computes this set. */
export struct Hoisted { Tok name; Ty type; Span at; }

/**
 * What the pass emits for `gen<Y> R f(A a)`: **two declarations where there was one.**
 *
 * A plan rather than a tree, which is what the shipped compiler's `asyncplan.wac` is called and is
 * the shape a pass that *emits* declarations has to take — noticed in the previous version of this
 * file before the reason was.
 */
export struct Plan {
  /** The invented struct: a resume tag and a field per hoisted local. */
  StructDecl machine;
  /** The rewritten `f`, which now constructs a `machine` and returns it. */
  Func driver;
  /** `step`, the original body cut at each suspension under a `match (this.at)`. */
  Func step;
}

/**
 * Plan the lowering of one generator.
 *
 * Answers a `Plan` rather than `Decl[]` because the three have different jobs and a caller that
 * wants to check the machine against the driver should not have to search an array for it. `emit`
 * flattens them.
 */
export Plan plan(Func gen, Vec<Hoisted> hoisted) { … }

/** The plan as declarations, in the order a single-pass resolver needs: the struct first. */
export Decl[] emit(const Plan p) { … }

/**
 * Every `gen` function in a program.
 *
 * **The previous version of this file said this one could not be written, and that was wrong.** §2.
 */
export Vec<Func> generatorsIn(Decl[] decls) {
  Vec<Func> out = Vec.create();
  for (Decl d in decls) {
    match (d.kind) {
      // Rebuilt with named fields, not `Func(name, ret, params, body, exported, typeParams,
      // isAsync)` — writing the positional form here, in the file arguing against it, is the mistake
      // this directory has now made three times by cataloguing a shape and then producing it.
      Func { name, ret, params, body, exported, typeParams, isAsync }: {
        if (isGen(ret)) {
          out.push(Func {
            name: name, ret: ret, params: params, body: body,
            exported: exported, typeParams: typeParams, isAsync: isAsync,
          });
        }
      }
      StructDecl { methods, .. }: { for (Func m in methods) { if (isGen(m.ret)) { out.push(m); } } }
      EnumDecl { .. }:  { }
      UnionDecl { .. }: { }
      Typedef { .. }:   { }
      Import { .. }:    { }
      ReExport { .. }:  { }
    }
  }
  return out;
}

/** Whether a return type is `gen<Y> R`. */
bool isGen(Ty t) { … }

// ## 1. Three lowerings, three levels, and only the third leaves the file's own vocabulary
//
// | | rewrites | needs |
// |---|---|---|
// | `try` | a statement list into a nested one | statements — `./stmt.wac` |
// | `union` | a type into an enum of one-field variants | types |
// | `gen` | a function into a struct **and** a function | declarations — `./decl.wac`, now |
//
// The three were costed together in `../../../TECHNICAL.md` as *the desugarings are easy and the
// parser is the work*, and on that measure they are the same size. They are not: two are tree-to-tree
// at a level the tree already has, and the third **adds a top-level declaration to the module**,
// which changes the export table, the type table and the order things are checked in. Not an
// argument against it — the shipped compiler does exactly this in `asyncsynth.wac` — but *"three
// lowerings"* is two lowerings and a code generator, and nothing had said so.
//
// ## 2. I said this pass was half-blocked and it is not, and the reason is worth more than the claim
//
// The version of this file committed an hour ago said `generatorsIn` could not be written: a `match`
// over a union matches by type, and `@/packages/box/src/gunzip.wac` records that matching by type
// does not bind. `../../../QUESTIONS.md` carried it, `./decl.wac` carried it, and it was wrong.
//
// `../../../GRAMMAR.ebnf` has **two** pattern languages and I read one:
//
//     binding_list   = binding , { "," , binding } , [ "," ] ;
//     binding        = IDENT | "is" , type ;
//     arm_payload    = "(" , [ binding_list ] , ")" | "{" , field_pattern , "}" ;
//     field_pattern  = ".." | IDENT , { "," , IDENT } , [ "," , ".." ] , [ "," ] ;
//
// The parenthesised form binds by **position** and can match by type without binding. The **brace
// pattern** binds by **field name** — `./walk.wac` uses it in every arm and is the file that argued
// for it. And `match_arm` is `IDENT , [ arm_payload ]`, which does not care whether the `IDENT` names
// an enum variant or a union member. So `Func { name, ret, .. }:` is grammatical, and `generatorsIn`
// above is written with it.
//
// The rule that actually holds is narrower and explains both cases at once:
//
// > **The brace pattern binds field names, so an arm can read its subject exactly when the subject
// > has fields.** An enum variant with a named payload has them. A union member that is a *struct*
// > has them. A union member that is itself a *union* does not — which is `gunzip`'s
// > `Err(is Corrupt):`, where `Corrupt` is eight members and there is nothing to name.
//
// So the gap is real and it is about **nested unions**, not about unions. I generalised from the one
// case that had been written to the case in front of me, and the two differ in the one property that
// matters. `./decl.wac`'s union-of-named-structs is unblocked in both directions: constructions are
// named and checked, and arms read by field name.
//
// **What the mistake cost is the interesting part.** Three commits carried it, and each restated it
// more confidently than the last — *a small gap*, then *load-bearing*, then *it splits a pass in
// half*. Nothing checked it, because the check is one grep of a grammar file in the same repository
// and the claim was about a construct's **absence**, which a targeted grep cannot find. Reading the
// production whole is the only thing that would have worked, and it is nineteen lines.
//
// ## 3. `yield` needs no inference and `await` does, which is a fact about the signature
//
// `packages/wacc/src/asyncplan.wac` declines a bare `await e;`, and its reason is exact:
//
// > **Both are the shapes that name the ticket's type**, which is not a coincidence: the machine
// > keeps the ticket it suspended at in a slot of type `Pending<X>`, and the only way to know `X`
// > from the AST alone is to have it written. `T x = await e;` says `T`, and `return await e;` says
// > the function's own return type. A bare `await e;` says neither, so it is declined here rather
// > than guessed at.
//
// **`yield e;` always names its type, because `gen<Y>` is in the signature.** So the case that forces
// `async` to refuse two of its three statement shapes does not arise for a generator: every `yield`
// in a body yields a `Y`, written once at the top, and a bare `yield e;` is the ordinary form rather
// than the declined one.
//
// Which inverts the intuition the two constructs invite. `gen` looks like the harder feature — it
// suspends *and* produces a value — and it is the easier lowering, because the extra thing it does is
// the thing that is declared. `async` produces nothing and must infer where `gen` reads.
//
// ## 4. The `Waiting` arm has to be deleted, which is the `never` rule again
//
// A synchronous `gen<Y> R f()` blocks on nothing, so its machine answers `Step<never, Y, R>` and its
// caller's `match` has two arms. That is `core/coroutine.wac`'s claim — *"a slot typed `never`
// removes its arm"* — and the lowering is where it has to be true: the pass emits a `match` over a
// three-variant enum with two arms, and either the checker accepts that at this instantiation or the
// pass emits a third arm that cannot be reached.
//
// Same operation as `union<never, E>` reducing to `E`. Third site, and the one where the rule is not
// a convenience: an unreachable `Waiting` arm in generated code has to *do* something, and the only
// honest thing is `trap`, which puts an unreachable trap in every generator in the program.
//
// ## 5. What could not be written
//
// **`generatorsIn` — see §2.** It is the first function in this directory that is blocked rather than
// elided, and the distinction matters: a `{ … }` body here means *the body is not the argument*, and
// this one means *there is no body that compiles*.
//
// **The hoisted set is computed and this file takes it as a parameter.** `asyncplan.wac` answers
// *which locals outlive a suspension* already, with its own tests — `ok suspends=1 hoist=3`. A
// generator needs exactly that and nothing more. `plan` takes a `Vec<Hoisted>` rather than computing
// it, which is right and means this file cannot be run without the pass that is not here.
//
// **Nothing says the resume tag's range.** `at` is an `i32` whose legal values are *the number of
// suspension points in this body*, known when the machine is built and never afterwards. So
// `match (this.at)` needs a `default:` that cannot happen — §4's `Waiting` arriving in the same
// function for a different reason, and one answer fixes both: a refinement of an integer, which
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has open.
//
// **`Plan` has three declarations and nothing says they must be emitted together.** `emit` orders
// them — struct first, for a single-pass resolver — and a caller can take `p.driver` alone and
// produce a program that references a type nobody declared. Same shape as `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s
// *paired calls over hidden state*, at a level where the pairing is between two things a **compiler**
// emits, so nothing outside this file could ever notice the pair was broken.
